---
// src/components/OtherIslandSection.astro
//
// Closing full-bleed illustration for the bottom of the page — not
// part of the flanking-islands family (ArtIslandSection /
// ComputerIslandSection / EducationIslandSection /
// AchievementIslandSection). No text, no mouse-repel, no bob loop —
// just one image that always spans the full viewport width, edge to
// edge, regardless of screen size.
//
// "Full viewport width no matter what" is done with the standard
// break-out-of-the-container trick:
//   width: 100vw; position: relative; left: 50%; right: 50%;
//   margin-left: -50vw; margin-right: -50vw;
// `width: 100%` alone would only match the width of whatever contains
// it. Right now nothing in Layout.astro constrains that (it's a bare
// `<slot />` in `<body>`), so `100%` would likely look identical today
// — but the 100vw break-out doesn't depend on that staying true, so
// this keeps working even if a max-width wrapper gets added around
// the page later. `overflow-x: hidden` on the section guards against
// the one common side effect of this trick: 100vw includes the
// vertical scrollbar's width, which can introduce a few px of
// horizontal overflow on some browsers.
//
// Height is deliberately NOT constrained — it's `height: auto`, so
// the image keeps its own aspect ratio at whatever height that
// implies once stretched to full width. Your source file is a very
// tall/portrait image (3840 x 6320), so at full viewport width this
// will render quite tall (on a 1440px-wide screen, roughly 2370px
// tall) — that's inherent to the source image's proportions, not a
// bug; let me know if you actually want it cropped to a shorter band
// instead of showing the whole illustration.
//
// IMPORTANT — file size: the uploaded SVG is ~45MB. It's not really a
// vector image; it's a single base64-encoded PNG wrapped in an <svg>
// (a `<use>` pointing at an embedded `<image>`), so the file weighs as
// much as that PNG does and won't scale crisply the way a real vector
// would either. 45MB shipped to every visitor at the end of a CV page
// is a lot — enough to make the page feel broken on a slow connection.
// Two things worth doing when you get a chance:
//   1. Re-export it as a compressed .webp or .avif (or a well-optimized
//      .jpg) instead of a base64 PNG inside an SVG — that alone could
//      plausibly take it from 45MB to a few hundred KB with no visible
//      quality loss for a photographic/painterly illustration like
//      this.
//   2. Since it's the very last thing on the page, `loading="lazy"` (already
//      added below) means it won't compete with anything above it for
//      bandwidth on initial load — but it'll still be a slow, heavy
//      request whenever it does load.
// Left the <img> pointed at the file as-is for now so this works today;
// swap the `image` prop to the smaller file once you have one, nothing
// else here needs to change.
//
// Usage:
//   <OtherIslandSection
//     id="section-closing"
//     image="/images/other_island.svg"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  image: string;
  alt?: string;
}

const { id, image, alt = "" } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="other-section" id={id}>
  <img class="other-section-image" src={image} alt={alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
</section>

<style>
  .other-section {
    position: relative;
    width: 100vw;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .other-section-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>